/'
Example demonstrating composition, aggregation, association, and dependency.
Composition is when the child object (or object being owned) cannot exist without the parent (owner).
   For example, composition is used to model the relationship between a house and a room. One house owns 1 or more rooms. The rooms
     do not exist independently of the house. Notation: A "num1" *-- "num2" B : description > 
Aggregation is when the child object (or object being owned) can exist without the parent (owner).
   For example, aggregation is used to model the relationship between a person who owns a house. A person can own a house, but the house exists
      without the person. Notation: A "num1" o-- "num2" B : description >
Association is a general relationship when there is no ownership or lifecycle dependency, but the relationship is permanent (stored).
   For example, association is used to model the relationship between a person who lives in a house.
      Notation: A "num1" -- "num2" B : description >
Dependency (or dependence) is a the weakest relationship where one class temporarily uses another.
   For example, dependence is used to model the relationship between a mail carrier and a house
      Notation: A "num1" .-> B : description >

    

'/

@startuml
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
hide circle

class House {
  -address: String
  -yearBuilt: int
  -squareFootage: double
  -rooms: Set<Room>
  -residents: Set<Person>
  --
  +House(address: String, yearBuilt: int, squareFootage: double)
  +addRoom(name: String, area: double, floorNumber: int): Room
  +getAddress(): String
  +getRooms(): Set<Room>
  ~addResident(person: Person): void
  ~removeResident(person: Person): void
  +getResidents(): Set<Person>
}

class Room {
  -name: String
  -area: double
  -floorNumber: int
  --
  ~Room(name: String, area: double, floorNumber: int)
  +getName(): String
  +getArea(): double
}

class Person {
  -firstName: String
  -lastName: String
  -residences: Set<House>
  -ownedHouses: Set<House>
  --
  +Person(firstName: String, lastName: String)
  +getFullName(): String
  +moveIn(house: House): void
  +moveOut(house: House): void
  +getResidences(): Set<House>
  +buyHouse(house: House): void
  +sellHouse(house: House): void
  +getOwnedHouses(): Set<House>
  +toString(): String
}

class MailCarrier {
  -employeeId: String
  --
  +MailCarrier(employeeId: String)
  +deliverMail(destination: House): void
}

note right of MailCarrier::deliverMail
  dependency: The MailCarrier does not store 
  the House as a variable. It just temporarily 
  uses it as a parameter to do a job.
end note

note right of House::addRoom
  composition: this is the ONLY way to create a Room.
  A Room cannot exist apart from a House.
end note

note right of Room::Room
  composition: this is a package-private constructor;
  a Room can only be created inside a House
end note

note right of Person::moveIn
  Creates a two-way association; The Person becomes a resident
  of the House, and the House becomes a residence of the Person,
  but neither own the other one. 
end note

note right of Person::buyHouse
  aggregation: House is created via 'new House(...)'
  independently of any owner, and buying/selling
  just changes which Person, if any, references it
end note

' Composition: enforced via factory method + package-private constructor
House "1" *-- "1..*" Room : contains >
note on link
  Composition is denoted by a filled in diamond
end note

' Aggregation: a House can exist before, after, or without a Person owning it
Person "0..1" o-- "0..*" House : owns >
note on link
  Aggregation is denoted by an open diamond
end note

' Association: no ownership or lifecycle dependency
Person "0..*" -- "0..*" House : lives in >
note on link
  Association is denoted by a solid line with no diamonds
end note


' Dependency: temporary usage with a dashed arrow
MailCarrier .–> House : delivers to >
note on link
  Dependency is denoted by a dotted line
end note

@enduml



